<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrew Carnegie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrew Carnegie was a Scottish-American industrialist and philanthropist who led the expansion of the American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industry in the late 19th century. Born in Dunfermline, he emigrated with his parents in 1848, worked in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mill and as a telegrapher, and invested in railroads, bridges and oil derricks. He built the Carnegie Steel Company in Pittsburgh and sold it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-p-morgan">J. P. Morgan</a> in 1901, when it formed the basis of U.S. Steel. In his 1889 article The Gospel of Wealth he called on the rich to use their wealth to improve society, and in the last 18 years of his life he gave away around $350 million, funding libraries, Carnegie Hall and the Peace Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrew-carnegie.jpg" alt="Andrew Carnegie" width="330" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Theodore C. Marceau · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrew_Carnegie,_by_Theodore_Marceau_(cropped)_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carnegie was born in Dunfermline to William and Margaret Morrison Carnegie, in a weaver's cottage with one main room shared with a neighbouring family. His father had a successful business and owned several looms. He was educated at the Free School in Dunfermline. In 1849 he became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> messenger boy in the Pittsburgh office of the Ohio Telegraph Company on his uncle's recommendation. He memorised the locations of the city's businesses and the faces of important men, and learned to read incoming signals by ear without the paper slip.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1853 Thomas A. Scott of the Pennsylvania Railroad employed him as a secretary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> operator at $4.00 a week. Before the Civil War he arranged a merger between Woodruff's company and George Pullman's sleeping-car firm, which proved profitable, and in 1864 he invested early in the Columbia Oil Company in Venango County. The wartime demand for iron made Pittsburgh a centre of production, and Carnegie helped establish a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> rolling mill.</p><p>He made his fortune in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, controlling the most extensive integrated iron and steel operations ever owned by an individual in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. One of his two great innovations was cheap mass production by adopting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessemer-process">Bessemer process</a>. In 1892 the Homestead Strike, a bloody confrontation lasting 143 days, centred on his company's main plant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>. He sold Carnegie Steel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-p-morgan">J. P. Morgan</a> in 1901 for $303,450,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1868, aged 33, he wrote a memo to himself that the amassing of wealth was one of the worse species of idolatry, though he began his giving in earnest only in 1881, with a library for Dunfermline. His Gospel of Wealth (1889) called on the rich to use their fortunes for society. He funded Carnegie Hall and the Peace Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> and gave away about $350 million. He opposed the annexation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and joined the American Anti-Imperialist League in 1898. He died of bronchial pneumonia at Lenox, Massachusetts, on 11 August 1919.</p><p>The biographer Joseph Wall suggested that giving away his money may have justified how he had made it. The historian Richard White argued that in disposing of his wealth Carnegie also sought to justify the existing social order.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andrew Carnegie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
